You want to add a link to every post on your website that points to the original URL of the post. This is a common practice to provide users with a direct link to a specific post.

To achieve this, you'll need to modify your Nginx configuration and add a new directive to your existing `location /` block. Here's the modified code:
```server {
    # ... (rest of your configuration remains the same)

    # index.html fallback
    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.html index.htm;
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;

        # Add a new directive to generate links to individual posts
        var post_link;
        if ($uri ~* "^/posts/([0-9]+)/?$") {
            set $post_link "https://www.askfundu.com/posts/$1";
        }
        add_header Link "<$post_link>; rel=post";
    }

    # ... (rest of your configuration remains the same)
}
```
Here's what's changed:

1. We added a new variable `post_link` that will hold the link to the individual post.
2. We used a regular expression (`^/posts/([0-9]+)?/$`) to match URL patterns that correspond to individual post pages (e.g., `/posts/123`, `/posts/abc`, etc.). The `( [0-9]+ )` part captures the post ID or slug.
3. We set the `post_link` variable to the formatted link using the captured group and the base URL.
4. We added a new `add_header` directive to include the `post` link in the HTML header. The format is `"<$post_link>; rel=post"`.

This will generate a link like `<https://www.askfundu.com/posts/123>` for every post on your website.

If you want to exclude certain URLs from being linked, you can modify the regular expression accordingly. For example, if you want to exclude URLs that start with `/posts/old/`, you can add a negative lookahead to the regular expression: `^/posts/(?!old/)([0-9]+)?/$`.

Additionally, if you have a custom post permalink structure, you may need to adjust the regular expression accordingly.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

